wifi: mt76: mt7615: credit tx status to the rate set that was in use

Tx status picks one of the two rate sets by comparing the time of the
first transmission with the time of the newest rate set update: a frame
first sent within one second after the update is credited to the newest
set, any other frame to the previous one. A frame first sent more than
a second after the update used the newest set too, so with sparse
traffic its status went to a rate set that was no longer in use. With
a frame every 3 seconds, 75-78% of the tx status reports between two
MT7615 were credited to the previous set.

Credit a frame to the previous set only if it was first sent before the
newest update.

--- a/mt7615/mac.c
+++ b/mt7615/mac.c
@@ -1590,6 +1590,7 @@ static bool __mt7615_fill_txs(struct mt7
 	bool ampdu, cck = false;
 	bool rs_idx;
 	u32 rate_set_tsf;
+	s32 age;
 	u32 final_rate, final_rate_flags, final_nss, txs;
 
 	txs = le32_to_cpu(txs_data[1]);
@@ -1628,10 +1629,14 @@ static bool __mt7615_fill_txs(struct mt7
 		goto out;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A frame first sent before the newest rate set update used the
+	 * previous rate set, one sent after it the newest set, however long
+	 * ago that update was.
+	 */
 	rate_set_tsf = READ_ONCE(sta->rate_set_tsf);
-	rs_idx = !((u32)(le32_get_bits(txs_data[4], MT_TXS4_F0_TIMESTAMP) -
-			 rate_set_tsf) < 1000000);
-	rs_idx ^= rate_set_tsf & BIT(0);
+	age = le32_get_bits(txs_data[4], MT_TXS4_F0_TIMESTAMP) - rate_set_tsf;
+	rs_idx = (age < 0) ^ (rate_set_tsf & BIT(0));
 	rs = &sta->rateset[rs_idx];
 
 	mt7615_txs_stats_set(dev, sta, txs_data, rate_set_tsf, rs_idx,
